{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>When a man takes a woman and cohabits with her.</b> 'And cohabits with her' — and there are two things: money and intercourse. For betrothal by money, 'taking' is only by money, as it is said (Genesis 23:13): ״נתתי כסף השדה קח ממני״. And there is also another acquisition by document, from what is written in this perashah: ״וצאה מביתו והלכה והיתה לאיש אחר,״ and the Sages, of blessed memory, derived by analogy: juxtapose 'becoming' (הויה) to 'going out' (יציאה) — just as 'going out' is by document, so too 'becoming' is by document. And thus there are three things, which are three modes of acquisition by which a woman is acquired, and so we have learned: a woman is acquired in three ways — by money, by document, and by intercourse. And this gezerah shavah of 'taking, taking' — we adjudicate capital cases on its basis, for a na'arah who commits adultery is exempt, but if she was betrothed by money alone she is liable to stoning. And since the gezerah shavah is one of the thirteen middot by which the Torah is interpreted and it is from the very body of the Torah, we rely upon it to adjudicate capital cases, and all of it requires witnesses — whether for betrothal by money or for betrothal by intercourse." "<b>A scroll of cutting off (sefer keritut).</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): this scroll cuts off between him and her, so that there be no bond with him in any matter all the days of her life. For if he said to her, ״This is your get on condition that you not drink wine forever״ or ״all the days of my life,״ this is not keritut; but if he said ״all the days of your life,״ this is keritut, since it is possible that he dies first and she is freed from his condition. And the same applies to all conditions, provided that no condition be written in the get, but rather stated orally or in a separate document." "<b>And by way of the Midrash.</b> 'A scroll of cutting off' must contain twelve lines, like a Torah scroll, in which a person must leave blank at the end of Genesis four lines, and at the end of Exodus four lines, and at the end of Leviticus four lines. And at the end of Numbers Sinai, it is not counted toward the reckoning, for it is reckoned as the last book, because 'These are the words' is a recurring matter, and if so, there are twelve lines in the Torah scroll. And Onkelos translated ספר כריתות as גט פטורין. And because the get has the status of a book, therefore it is called a 'book' (sefer) and requires twelve lines, corresponding to the numerical value of ג״ט." "<b>And if</b> you give insight into the matter of the get that is called a 'book' (sefer), you will find that the wisdom of the Torah required a man to divorce his wife by means of a book (sefer), because the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world with twenty-two letters, above and below, and paired the letters and weighed them and exchanged them and combined them, and with them formed the entire world and all creation and all speech. And there is no weighing except by way of pairing (zivug), and by way of the weighing of letters the Holy One, blessed be He, weighed all created beings — this one is fitting to be the mate of that one — and how can all existent beings stand each opposite the other, whether among the supernal beings or the lower ones, whether in souls or in bodies, as the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: 'the daughter of so-and-so for so-and-so,' and even across the sea. And since the joining of created beings and their pairing came about through letters, therefore the Torah commanded that the separation too be through letters, to declare and make known that the essence of pairing and joining from days of old, at the creation of the world, was through letters."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:50:52.002399701Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}